From 685baf8c717e6f0712e199a84241b104034a8ee3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikolajQ <mikolaj.q@wp.pl>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 10:58:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] HostEmulationManager: Only bind the payment service while NFC
 is on

The preferred payment service is bound persistently to cut the latency
of the first APDU. The binding is made on boot and whenever the
preferred payment service changes, but it is never dropped when NFC is
turned off, so it outlives the only state in which it can be used.

The binding comes from com.android.nfc with BIND_AUTO_CREATE, which
keeps the payment app at a visible oom_adj around the clock. Its process
is never cached or frozen, and its jobs, alarms and network traffic are
not deferred like those of other background apps. Users who keep NFC
off and enable it only to pay get this cost all day for nothing.

Unbind the payment service when NFC is disabled and bind it again when
NFC is enabled. Changes of the preferred payment service while NFC is
off are picked up on the next enable, as the service is looked up from
the AID cache at that point.

Test: OnePlus 9 Pro, banking app with an HCE payment service holding
      the wallet role, NFC off: before, its process sat at oom_adj 100
      (procState 6) bound by com.android.nfc for days.

Assisted-by: LLM
Change-Id: Id16e2a9aa90a23140d1f24baa3b0d9205c99620d
